  "description": "This is an application to install a Metal 15,000ltr Fuel Tank at our site in Barking. \nOverall External Dimensions: 4050mm (L) x 2500mm (W) x 2900mm (H) this style of tank resembles a ISO sea container.    \nThe reason for installing this tank is that we are switching over from Diesel to HVO Biofuel to fuel our fleet of vehicles. \nThis will make a significant reduction to our carbon footprint - figures suggest that this switch could reduce our carbon emissions by up to 90%.\nWe have already removed the old 18,000ltr Diesel Tank from our site – which had been out of action for at least as long as we have been at this premises. Our plan is to install a modern, safer & double-skin bunded tank.   \n\nThe new tank will be placed at least 6 metres away from the neighbouring building – to comply with Regulations for fuel tanks above 3500ltrs capacity. The tank will also be more than 6 metres away from the pedestrian pathway and access road leading into the industrial estate (Lyon Business Park). \nWe will be installing traffic protection in the form of bollards to prevent any kind of impact from vehicles.\n\nAs the tank itself is double-skinned, it greatly reduces the risk of any fuel spills caused by tank defects. \nThe tank has sensors inside which will alert key members of staff of any unexpected drops in fuel amounts (alerting us to any leaks or suspected theft). \nThe Refuelling area will also be fitted with floodlights and cameras. The tank will not be visible from the road side as it will be screened off.\nThe Fuel line is also fitted with standard petrol station forecourt safety features such as: \nAutomatic Stop - if trigger isn’t depressed. \nFilling Level Stop – won’t allow someone to overfill a vehicle.\nAutomatic Fuel Line Shut Off – in the event that someone drives away with the Filling gun still in the Truck Tank, which minimizes the amount of fuel spilt and retains the bulk of the fuel in the Feeder Tank.         \nWe will also be issued with bespoke drain covers and spill kits to be used in the unlikely event of any other kind of fuel spill.\n",
